---
registryId: temporal
---

{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is Temporal?

**Temporal is a durable execution platform for application processes that must keep
making progress across crashes, retries, deployments, and long waits.** You write a
Workflow as code. The Temporal Service stores the Workflow's ordered Event History,
and your Worker processes use that history to rebuild state and continue.

Temporal matters when a business process spans several unreliable steps: charging a
payment, waiting days for approval, provisioning infrastructure, or coordinating a
multi-service order. Without durable execution, application teams often invent status
tables, polling loops, retry jobs, and recovery scripts that are difficult to keep
consistent.

{% callout variant="info" %}
The core invariant is **same history, same Workflow decisions**. Workflow code must be
deterministic so replay emits commands compatible with the recorded Event History.
External I/O belongs in Activities, whose retries require an idempotency strategy.
{% /callout %}

Temporal preserves orchestration progress. It does **not** make an arbitrary payment,
email, or database mutation exactly once.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Build the durable execution mental model

Temporal does not continuously snapshot a Worker's process memory. A Workflow Task
runs code, emits Commands such as "schedule this Activity" or "start this Timer," and
the Temporal Service turns outcomes into durable Events. If a Worker disappears, a
later Worker re-executes the Workflow code against those Events to reconstruct state.

{% system-flow title="Temporal execution path" caption="The Service owns durable orchestration state. Application Workers own and execute Workflow and Activity code outside the Service." %}
{% system-node title="Client" eyebrow="Start or message" icon="activity" tone="blue" %}
Starts a Workflow with a stable Workflow ID, then sends Signals or Updates and reads
state with Queries.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Temporal Service" eyebrow="History + routing" icon="database" tone="violet" %}
Persists Event History, timers, retry state, and task routing. It does not run your
business code.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Workflow Worker" eyebrow="Replay + decide" icon="workflow" tone="cyan" %}
Polls a Task Queue, rebuilds Workflow state from history, and emits the next
deterministic Commands.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Activity Worker" eyebrow="Perform I/O" icon="server" tone="green" %}
Calls databases, APIs, files, or models. The Activity result or failure is reported to
the Service and becomes part of the Workflow's progress.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### What replay does and does not repeat

- Workflow code can run many times while rebuilding state.
- A recorded timer does not wait again during replay.
- A recorded Activity result is returned from history; replay does not call the
  Activity implementation again.
- An Activity attempt can still run again when its external result succeeded but
  Temporal did not record completion before a timeout or Worker loss.
- Direct network calls, wall-clock APIs, random branching, and mutable global state in
  Workflow code can produce non-deterministic decisions.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Separate Workflows from Activities

The cleanest beginner rule is: **a Workflow decides; an Activity does**. Keep durable
business state and ordering in the Workflow. Put failure-prone interaction with the
outside world in Activities.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Workflow" eyebrow="Durable coordinator" icon="workflow" tone="blue" %}
Models business state, branching, timers, retries, compensation, messages, and child
coordination. It must use replay-safe SDK APIs and remain deterministic.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Activity" eyebrow="External operation" icon="activity" tone="amber" %}
Performs one well-defined unit of I/O. It can use normal libraries, but must tolerate
timeouts, cancellation, and execution by more than one attempt.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Worker" eyebrow="Your runtime" icon="server" tone="green" %}
Hosts Workflow and Activity implementations and polls Task Queues. Scale Workers by
measured task arrival, execution time, resource needs, and queue latency.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

Use a regular function for pure code organization inside one Workflow. Use an Activity
for an external operation. Use a Child Workflow only when the child needs its own
durable lifecycle, Event History, task routing, or independent cancellation policy.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/temporal/code/order-workflow.ts" language="typescript" title="A deterministic order Workflow with messages and a Child Workflow" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Trace replay through a failure

The critical boundary is not "did my function run?" It is "what did the Temporal
Service record, and what did the external system commit?" Select an incident, move
through the Event History, then change the replay and side-effect contracts.

{% interactive-block id="technology/temporal-replay-lab" dataFile="/api/content/technology/temporal/data/replay-scenarios.json" /%}

{% callout variant="warn" %}
An Activity is commonly **at least once** across attempts. Pass a stable business
operation ID to the external system, store the first result, and make later attempts
return that result or become a no-op. Reconciliation is still required when the
external system cannot enforce idempotency.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Route Tasks to capable Workers

A Task Queue is a logical routing name polled by Workers. Workflow Tasks advance
orchestration state; Activity Tasks execute external work. The Temporal Service keeps
durable Workflow state while Worker processes remain replaceable.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Name the capability" label="Queue contract" %}
Use a stable Task Queue name for a compatible Worker capability, such as
`orders-workflow-v1`, `payments`, or `gpu-transcode`.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Register implementations" label="Worker code" %}
A Worker polling that queue must contain the Workflow or Activity types that tasks on
the queue can request, plus the credentials and network access those Activities need.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Measure pickup delay" label="Capacity" %}
Watch schedule-to-start latency, task throughput, poller count, Worker saturation, and
backlog age. A growing queue usually means arrival exceeds available execution
capacity.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Scale or isolate" label="Operations" %}
Add Workers, reduce per-task cost, apply Worker tuning, or split resource-incompatible
Activities. Do not use queue proliferation as a substitute for clear ownership.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

If queue delivery and worker-pool concepts are new, review
[Message Queues](/reference/message-queues) before tuning production Task Queues.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="rose" %}
## Bound failure with retries, timeouts, heartbeats, and cancellation

These controls answer different questions. Configure them from the business operation,
not from one global default.

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="Retry Policy" eyebrow="Should another attempt run?" icon="repeat" tone="violet" %}
Use backoff for transient failures, cap delay and attempts when the business deadline
requires it, and mark validation or authorization failures non-retryable.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Start-to-Close" eyebrow="Is one attempt stuck?" icon="timer" tone="amber" %}
Bounds one Activity Task attempt. Set it longer than a healthy attempt but short enough
to detect a crashed or wedged Worker.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Schedule-to-Close" eyebrow="Is the operation too old?" icon="timer" tone="rose" %}
Bounds the entire Activity Execution across queueing, attempts, and retry backoff. It
expresses the overall business time budget.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Heartbeat Timeout" eyebrow="Is long work progressing?" icon="activity" tone="green" %}
Detects missing progress for a long Activity. Heartbeat details can checkpoint
application progress for the next attempt.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Cancellation is cooperative

- A Workflow receives cancellation through SDK cancellation scopes and should decide
  which cleanup or compensation must still run.
- A long Activity must heartbeat to receive cancellation promptly.
- A request to cancel does not prove an external system rolled back work already
  committed.
- Compensation is a new business action, not time travel. It needs its own retries,
  idempotency, audit record, and manual-repair path.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/temporal/code/payment-activities.ts" language="typescript" title="Idempotent payment and heartbeat checkpoint Activities" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Make every external side effect replay-safe

Temporal records orchestration, but the Activity-to-external-system boundary can be
ambiguous. A Worker may commit a charge and fail before reporting completion. The
Service eventually times out that attempt and dispatches another.

Use all applicable controls:

- Derive an idempotency key from durable business identity, such as
  `workflowId + operationName + operationVersion`, not from an attempt number.
- Ask the destination to enforce that key and return the original result on duplicate
  requests.
- When you own the database, commit the business mutation and idempotency receipt in
  one transaction with a unique constraint.
- Record provider IDs and reconcile unknown outcomes instead of blindly retrying a
  non-idempotent call.
- Design compensations for committed work that cannot be deduplicated or reversed
  atomically.
- Keep attempt-local logs distinct from business-success metrics so retries do not
  inflate completed-order counts.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Communicate with running Workflows

Treat a Workflow as a durable stateful service with a stable identity. Signals, Queries,
and Updates have different acceptance and response contracts.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Signal" eyebrow="Asynchronous write" icon="route" tone="blue" %}
Delivers a message that can change Workflow state. The sender does not wait for a
handler result. Use it for notifications, approvals, or eventual commands.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Query" eyebrow="Read current state" icon="search" tone="cyan" %}
Reads Workflow state without adding an Event History entry. Query handlers must not
mutate state or block while waiting for a future condition.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Update" eyebrow="Tracked write with result" icon="check" tone="green" %}
Validates and changes Workflow state while allowing the caller to wait for a result or
failure. Accepted Updates add durable history.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

Message handlers can interleave with the main Workflow and with one another. Validate
state transitions, keep handlers short, await unfinished handlers before completing or
continuing as new, and use message IDs when clients may retry submission.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Split lifecycles and control Event History growth

Start with one Workflow when the process is bounded. More Workflow boundaries add
separate histories, IDs, message paths, cancellation behavior, and operational
ownership.

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="One Workflow" eyebrow="Default" icon="workflow" tone="blue" %}
Best for a bounded process whose Activities and messages share one business lifecycle.
Regular functions can organize code without introducing distributed coordination.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Child Workflow" eyebrow="Independent durable lifecycle" icon="layers" tone="violet" %}
Use when a child needs separate routing, history, retries, identity, or parent-close
behavior. A Child is not merely a helper function.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Continue-As-New" eyebrow="Fresh run, same chain" icon="repeat" tone="green" %}
Closes the current run successfully and starts a new run with the same Workflow ID, a
new Run ID, passed-forward state, and a fresh Event History.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

History grows with Activities, timers, Signals, Updates, retries, and children. Watch
history length and size before platform limits become an incident. Use the SDK's
continue-as-new suggestion, pass only the state needed by the next run, and wait for
message handlers to finish. Ongoing Child Workflows do not automatically move into a
parent's new run; choose the Parent Close Policy and handoff deliberately.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Deploy Workflow code without breaking replay

Activity implementation changes usually affect the next Activity attempt. Workflow
code changes are more sensitive because open executions can replay histories produced
by older code.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Replay representative histories" label="Pre-merge" %}
Export scrubbed histories from realistic executions and run the new Workflow code
against them in CI. A passing unit test does not prove replay compatibility.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Version the Worker deployment" label="Routing" %}
Prefer Worker Versioning so pinned Workflows stay on compatible code while approved
auto-upgrade Workflows move through ramping and current deployment versions.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Patch unavoidable branches" label="Compatibility" %}
When Worker Versioning is unavailable or an in-code migration is required, use the
SDK's versioning or patch APIs so old histories and new executions choose recorded,
deterministic branches.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Drain with evidence" label="Rollout" %}
Observe replay failures, Workflow Task failures, task pickup latency, error rates, and
old-version execution counts before retiring compatible Workers.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

Deploy Workers as independently scalable application processes with graceful shutdown.
The Temporal Service or Temporal Cloud coordinates them but does not ship or execute
your Workflow and Activity code.

{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/temporal/code/worker.ts" language="typescript" title="A Worker process with explicit routing and shutdown" /%}
{% code-block file="/api/content/technology/temporal/code/production-policy.yaml" language="yaml" title="Application-owned production policy, not Temporal server configuration" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="rose" %}
## Operate recovery as a measured control loop

Choose an incident, then change Worker capacity and recovery controls. The model keeps
four concerns separate: queue pressure, failure detection, side-effect safety, and
history or deployment compatibility.

{% interactive-block id="technology/temporal-operations-lab" dataFile="/api/content/technology/temporal/data/operations-scenarios.json" /%}

The numbers in this lab are planning inputs for the example system, not Temporal
product limits. Benchmark your Worker code and set an internal Event History budget
below the current service limits for your deployment.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Design visibility and namespace boundaries

Visibility lets operators list, filter, count, and search Workflow Executions. Add
Search Attributes for stable operational dimensions such as customer tier, region,
order status, or incident cohort. Do not put unbounded, secret, or high-cardinality
payload data into searchable metadata.

### Monitor the path users depend on

- Workflow completion latency, failure, cancellation, termination, and stuck-state age.
- Workflow Task and Activity Task schedule-to-start latency, execution latency,
  timeouts, retry attempts, and task failures.
- Poller presence, Worker saturation, sticky-cache behavior, graceful shutdown, and
  deployment-version adoption.
- Event History length and size, Continue-As-New rate, Signal and Update volume, and
  child counts.
- External business outcomes, idempotency conflicts, compensation failures, and
  reconciliation backlog.

A Namespace is a unit of isolation for Workflow IDs, Task Queues, retention, archival,
and operational configuration. Choose Namespaces for meaningful environment,
ownership, security, compliance, or failure-isolation boundaries. A Namespace can
still contain many applications, so teams sharing one must coordinate Workflow IDs,
Task Queue names, Search Attributes, quotas, and access.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="green" %}
## Test recovery before production needs it

Use a test pyramid that exercises Temporal behavior rather than only pure functions.

- Unit-test pure decision code and Activity adapters.
- Test Activities with mocked context, including heartbeat details, cancellation,
  idempotency conflicts, provider timeouts, and non-retryable errors.
- Run Workflow integration tests with mocked Activities and time skipping for timers,
  retry windows, escalation, cancellation, Signals, Queries, and Updates.
- Replay saved Event Histories against every Workflow-code change.
- Run end-to-end tests with a test server, real Workers, and controlled external
  sandboxes.
- In staging, kill Workers during Activity execution, remove all pollers from a Task
  Queue, delay dependencies, interrupt deployments, and force Continue-As-New.
- Practice operational repair: reset or terminate only with a written decision,
  preserve audit evidence, reconcile external effects, and verify the user-visible
  outcome.

{% callout variant="tip" %}
Recovery is successful only when durable Workflow state and external business state
agree. A green Workflow status can still hide a duplicate charge, missing email, or
unreconciled provider result.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

